## Add Databricks group support to permission resolution

Context: Granting a Databricks group CAN_MANAGE or CAN_USE on the App was
silently ignored. Group rows were already stored and rendered end to end (App
ACL parser, Teams matrix, `domain_permissions`), but nothing resolved them at
request time, so a user who only had access through a group hit the
access-denied page. Three defects: `is_admin` compared ACL principals to the
caller's e-mail only, so a `group_name` could never match; `_get_user_groups`
searched SCIM `/Users` with the app service-principal token, which normally
lacks the workspace-admin rights that endpoint requires, so it 403'd and
returned no groups; and `_resolve_domain_entry_role` returned the first
matching entry rather than the most privileged one. Backend-only fix — no UI
or behaviour change anywhere.

Changes:

1. src/back/core/databricks/WorkspaceService.py
   New `get_current_user_groups()` reading SCIM `/Me`, which any user may call
   for themselves, unlike the `/Users` search.
2. src/back/core/databricks/DatabricksClient.py
   Passthrough for `get_current_user_groups()`.
3. src/back/objects/registry/PermissionService.py
   `_get_user_groups` now takes `user_token` and prefers SCIM `/Me` with the
   caller's own forwarded token, falling back to the SP `/Users` query for
   contexts with no forwarded token. Split into `_groups_via_scim_me` /
   `_groups_via_scim_users`, which return `None` on failure so a transient 403
   is no longer cached as "no groups" for the whole 5-minute window.
4. src/back/objects/registry/PermissionService.py
   `_check_admin_sdk` / `_check_admin_rest` replaced by
   `_can_manage_principals_sdk` / `_can_manage_principals_rest`, returning the
   CAN_MANAGE principal list instead of a bool. Matching moved into one
   `_matches_principal` helper that tests the e-mail *and* every group the
   caller belongs to, removing the logic previously duplicated across both
   checks. Shared `_extract_can_manage` parses the ACL payload once.
5. src/back/objects/registry/PermissionService.py
   `_resolve_domain_entry_role` collects the direct user entry plus every
   matching group entry and returns the highest via `role_level`. SCIM is only
   consulted when the domain actually has group rows.
6. src/back/objects/domain/SettingsService.py
   `build_permissions_diag` reuses `_extract_can_manage` (dropping a third
   copy of the ACL walk) and reports `user_groups` and `group_is_manager`, so
   the diagnostic no longer says `email_is_manager: false` for someone who is
   an admin through a group.
7. documentation/deployment.md
   New "Users and Groups" subsection in section 4 plus an updated admin-
   detection description: group grants are inherited by members, the most
   privileged match wins, membership comes from the user's own token, and
   nested groups are not expanded.
8. tests/units/auth/test_permissions.py
   `TestIsAdmin` migrated to the new helpers and extended with group-admin,
   non-member, case-insensitivity, user-token precedence and all-paths-fail
   cases. New `TestExtractCanManage` and `TestGetUserGroups` classes. New
   max-role cases in `TestResolveDomainEntryRole`.
9. tests/units/core/test_workspace_service.py
   New `TestGetCurrentUserGroups` covering success, no groups, 403 and no auth.

Behaviour note: workspace administrators hold CAN_MANAGE on the app through
the built-in `admins` group, so they now resolve as OntoBricks Admins. This is
deliberate and consistent with how the user-level check already treats
inherited ACL entries. Nested (group-in-group) membership is not expanded;
only groups SCIM reports directly on the user account count.

